=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — that Laptop2 cannot reach Laptop3 on VLAN 10 through switch1 — does not exist in the live lab. A direct ICMP probe from Laptop2 (172.16.10.12) to Laptop3 (172.16.10.13) returned 3/3 packets with 0% loss and an ARP entry of REACHABLE, confirming full L2 and L3 reachability between the two hosts.

A thorough sweep of every layer found no mechanism for the reported symptom. switch1's Linux bridge (br_vlan10) has both Laptop2's port (eth3) and Laptop3's port (eth4) in the forwarding state with their MACs correctly learned in the FDB; neither port is blocked, err-disabled, or absent. The ebtables and nftables rule sets on switch1 contain only Docker NAT entries with ACCEPT default policies — no frame-dropping rules exist. On the host side, both laptops have correctly assigned /24 addresses within 172.16.10.0/24, matching gateway (172.16.10.1), and their eth0 interfaces are UP/LOWER_UP. The l2_snapshot found zero duplicate MAC addresses across all 32 devices, ruling out a MAC conflict. tc_snapshot showed no bandwidth throttling or corruption on any interface. The pressure_sweep found no overloaded device. The router6 drop rule on eth2 (the r6_r3 WAN link) that infra_sweep flagged is structurally unrelated to VLAN 10 intra-switch communication and does not affect the laptop2–laptop3 path in any way.

Root cause: No fault exists for the reported symptom; Laptop2 and Laptop3 communicate normally through switch1 on VLAN 10.

Fix: No corrective action is needed for this ticket. If the user continues to experience intermittent failures, capture a simultaneous ping log from both endpoints and record timestamps to distinguish transient host-level issues (e.g., a momentary ARP cache stale state) from a real switching fault.
=== END ===